WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.443
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:08.607
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.907 --> 00:00:16.227
Unit twenty-two: Global Health. In this unit, you will discuss how the world can protect health for everyone.

4
00:00:17.217 --> 00:00:18.961
pandemic preparedness.

5
00:00:19.661 --> 00:00:24.888
pandemic preparedness means being ready to respond quickly to a global outbreak.

6
00:00:25.188 --> 00:00:28.348
Pandemic preparedness saves lives and money.

7
00:00:29.048 --> 00:00:32.003
Your turn. Say: pandemic preparedness.

8
00:00:34.703 --> 00:00:36.366
pandemic preparedness.

9
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.199
universal health coverage.

10
00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:45.741
universal health coverage means access to health services for everyone without financial hardship.

11
00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:49.979
Thailand achieved universal health coverage in 2002.

12
00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:54.516
Your turn. Say: universal health coverage.

13
00:00:57.216 --> 00:00:58.995
universal health coverage.

14
00:00:59.983 --> 00:01:01.542
health inequity.

15
00:01:02.242 --> 00:01:06.157
health inequity means unfair differences in health between groups.

16
00:01:06.457 --> 00:01:10.001
Health inequity is visible between cities and villages.

17
00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:13.922
Your turn. Say: health inequity.

18
00:01:16.622 --> 00:01:18.308
health inequity.

19
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:20.898
neglected disease.

20
00:01:21.598 --> 00:01:27.464
neglected disease means a disease that affects poor people and receives little research funding.

21
00:01:27.764 --> 00:01:30.646
Dengue was long considered a neglected disease.

22
00:01:31.346 --> 00:01:35.217
Your turn. Say: neglected disease.

23
00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:39.545
neglected disease.

24
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.584
antimicrobial resistance.

25
00:01:43.284 --> 00:01:49.301
antimicrobial resistance means when bacteria and other germs stop responding to medicines.

26
00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:53.737
Antimicrobial resistance could make simple infections deadly.

27
00:01:54.437 --> 00:01:58.180
Your turn. Say: antimicrobial resistance.

28
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.031
antimicrobial resistance.

29
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:05.401
vaccine equity.

30
00:02:06.101 --> 00:02:10.527
vaccine equity means fair distribution of vaccines between countries.

31
00:02:10.827 --> 00:02:14.371
Vaccine equity was a major issue during COVID-19.

32
00:02:15.071 --> 00:02:18.118
Your turn. Say: vaccine equity.

33
00:02:20.818 --> 00:02:22.492
vaccine equity.

34
00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:25.320
health system capacity.

35
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:32.432
health system capacity means the ability of a health system to treat patients.

36
00:02:32.732 --> 00:02:35.474
The outbreak tested health system capacity.

37
00:02:36.174 --> 00:02:39.871
Your turn. Say: health system capacity.

38
00:02:42.571 --> 00:02:44.629
health system capacity.

39
00:02:45.617 --> 00:02:47.129
disease surveillance.

40
00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:51.384
disease surveillance means monitoring the spread of diseases.

41
00:02:51.684 --> 00:02:54.438
Disease surveillance detected the outbreak early.

42
00:02:55.138 --> 00:02:58.789
Your turn. Say: disease surveillance.

43
00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:03.012
disease surveillance.

44
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:06.283
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:06.583 --> 00:03:13.389
Rithy, by 2030, the world will have been talking about pandemic preparedness for a decade.

46
00:03:14.683 --> 00:03:17.925
Yet many countries still lack the capacity to respond.

47
00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:25.120
True. And antimicrobial resistance is a quieter but equally serious threat.

48
00:03:26.417 --> 00:03:29.008
Because people overuse antibiotics?

49
00:03:30.317 --> 00:03:37.300
Partly, and in farming too. By the time new drugs arrive, bacteria will have been adapting for years.

50
00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:43.233
What about vaccine equity? Poorer countries waited months during COVID-19.

51
00:03:44.517 --> 00:03:48.478
That was a clear example of health inequity on a global scale.

52
00:03:49.783 --> 00:03:55.010
So universal health coverage should be the long-term goal for every country.

53
00:03:56.317 --> 00:04:00.638
Grammar focus: Future perfect continuous in argument.

54
00:04:00.938 --> 00:04:06.862
In arguments, the future perfect continuous emphasises how long a problem will have continued.

55
00:04:07.162 --> 00:04:11.855
By the time new drugs arrive, bacteria will have been adapting for years.

56
00:04:12.155 --> 00:04:14.189
It adds urgency to your point.

57
00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:20.506
In arguments, this tense highlights how long a problem will have continued, adding urgency.

58
00:04:21.517 --> 00:04:24.541
Now read and listen. Health Without Borders.

59
00:04:24.841 --> 00:04:30.184
Diseases do not respect national borders, as COVID-19 clearly demonstrated.

60
00:04:31.483 --> 00:04:37.825
Strong pandemic preparedness requires disease surveillance, trained staff and emergency funding.

61
00:04:39.117 --> 00:04:43.984
Yet health system capacity varies enormously between countries.

62
00:04:45.283 --> 00:04:50.394
During the pandemic, wealthy nations secured vaccines months before poorer ones.

63
00:04:51.683 --> 00:04:56.051
This lack of vaccine equity prolonged the crisis for everyone.

64
00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:03.192
Another growing threat is antimicrobial resistance, which could make common infections untreatable.

65
00:05:04.483 --> 00:05:09.954
Neglected diseases such as dengue continue to affect millions in tropical regions.

66
00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:17.743
Universal health coverage is increasingly seen as the foundation of global health security.

67
00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.876
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:22.176 --> 00:05:25.685
When germs stop responding to medicines, it is blank.

69
00:05:25.985 --> 00:05:32.843
A, vaccine equity. B, antimicrobial resistance. C, disease surveillance.

70
00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.452
The answer is B: antimicrobial resistance.

71
00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:46.063
By 2030, she blank as a nurse for fifteen years.

72
00:05:46.363 --> 00:05:51.049
A, will be working. B, will have been working. C, has worked.

73
00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.524
The answer is B: will have been working.

74
00:06:00.017 --> 00:06:03.119
By the time we finish, we blank for ten hours.

75
00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:07.769
A, will have been travelling. B, will travel. C, travelled.

76
00:06:11.783 --> 00:06:14.479
The answer is A: will have been travelling.

77
00:06:15.983 --> 00:06:19.794
Fair distribution of vaccines between countries is blank.

78
00:06:20.094 --> 00:06:26.824
A, vaccine equity. B, health inequity. C, health system capacity.

79
00:06:30.817 --> 00:06:33.757
The answer is A: vaccine equity.

80
00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:39.862
According to the text, what is the foundation of global health security?

81
00:06:40.162 --> 00:06:46.393
A, private hospitals. B, universal health coverage. C, new vaccines.

82
00:06:50.383 --> 00:06:54.368
The answer is B: universal health coverage.

83
00:06:55.883 --> 00:07:01.682
Now it's your turn. Part one: What should be the top global health priority in the next decade?

84
00:07:06.982 --> 00:07:13.602
Part two: Choose one priority, give two reasons, and suggest one action for Cambodia.

85
00:07:19.902 --> 00:07:32.089
I'd choose antimicrobial resistance. It threatens basic medicine, and it is growing quietly. Cambodia could stop the sale of antibiotics without prescriptions and educate farmers about their use.

86
00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:35.538
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:42.360
Great work today! You learned eight new words and future perfect continuous in argument.

88
00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:47.240
Today's value is healthy body. Move your body every day.

89
00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:51.376
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

90
00:07:51.676 --> 00:07:54.747
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

